{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The destruction of the fourteen wells and the communal grain stores was a prohibited attack under Article 54(2) of Additional Protocol I because they are objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population and were destroyed for the purpose of denying them to civilians.",
      "reasoning": "Article 54(2) expressly forbids attacking, destroying, removing or rendering useless objects indispensable to civilian survival, such as drinking‑water installations and foodstuffs, when the purpose is to deny their sustenance value to the civilian population, \"whatever the motive\". The Drennick order explicitly aimed to leave nothing for the returning villagers, and the wells were poisoned and the grain stores burned, directly rendering these indispensable objects useless for the 6,000 civilians who depend on them. The factual matrix therefore satisfies each element of the prohibition.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54(2)",
          "quoted_text": "It is prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, such as ... drinking water installations and supplies ... for the specific purpose of denying them for their sustenance value to the civilian population or to the adverse Party, whatever the motive"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The claimed justification of \"imperative military necessity\" does not excuse the conduct because the derogation in Article 54(5) of Additional Protocol I applies only to a Party’s own territory under its control, not to occupied foreign territory.",
      "reasoning": "Paragraph 5 of Article 54 permits derogation \"by a Party to the conflict within such territory under its own control\" where required by imperative military necessity. The Velmir Valley was Calvaran territory occupied by Drennick forces; it was not territory under Drennick’s own sovereign control. Consequently, the exception cannot be invoked, and the destruction remains unlawful despite the asserted military necessity.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54",
          "quoted_text": "In recognition of the vital requirements of any Party to the conflict in the defence of its national territory against invasion, derogation from the prohibitions contained in paragraph 2 may be made by a Party to the conflict within such territory under its own control where required by imperative military necessity."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The exemption in Article 54(3) of Additional Protocol I does not apply because the wells and grain stores were not used for the sustenance of Drennick’s armed forces nor for direct support of military action, and the attack would inevitably cause starvation of the civilian population.",
      "reasoning": "Paragraph 3(a) allows attacks on objects used \"as sustenance solely for the members of its armed forces,\" and 3(b) permits attacks when the objects are used \"in direct support of military action,\" provided the attack does not leave civilians with inadequate food or water causing starvation. The facts show that the wells and stores were not used by Drennick troops (they relied on their own logistics) and were destroyed precisely to deny civilians any water or food, which would leave the population without the means to survive. Thus neither sub‑paragraph applies, and the conduct remains prohibited.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "54",
          "quoted_text": "The prohibitions in paragraph 2 shall not apply to such of the objects covered by it as are used by an adverse Party: (a) as sustenance solely for the members of its armed forces; or (b) if not as sustenance, then in direct support of military action, provided, however, that in no event shall actions against these objects be taken which may be expected to leave the civilian population with such inadequate food or water as to cause its starvation or force its movement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The conduct also breaches the broader prohibition on starvation of civilians as a method of warfare, embodied in Article 14 of Additional Protocol II, which mirrors the protection in Article 54 of Protocol I.",
      "reasoning": "Article 14 of Additional Protocol II states that starvation of civilians is prohibited and that it is therefore prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless objects indispensable to civilian survival for that purpose. Even though Protocol II applies primarily to non‑international armed conflicts, its rule reflects customary international humanitarian law and reinforces the absolute nature of the prohibition. The deliberate poisoning of wells and burning of grain stores to deprive civilians of water and food constitutes the very method of warfare outlawed by this provision.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_ii",
          "article": "14",
          "quoted_text": "Starvation of civilians as a method of combat is prohibited. It is therefore prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless, for that purpose, objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, such as ... drinking water installations and supplies ..."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

